## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to build a scalable, privacy-preserving ML pipeline that composes multiple AWS services (S3, Step Functions, Lambda, SageMaker) with governance and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven pipelines with S3 triggers
- Step Functions coordinating Lambda and SageMaker
- PII redaction in Lambda
- SageMaker inference integration for sentiment and topics
- Data governance: KMS encryption, least-privilege IAM, auditing
- Reliability: DLQ, retries, monitoring, cost controls

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda pseudocode for redacting PII from JSON input
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.body || '{}');
  if (data.email) data.email = '[REDACTED]';
  if (data.phone) data.phone = '[REDACTED]';
  return { redacted: true, data };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate redaction quality and model bias at scale?
- What strategies ensure latency targets under burst traffic?
- How would you enforce data-retention policies and revocation requests across services?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses end-to-end design of a scalable, privacy-conscious telemetry pipeline across accounts and regions, plus data cataloging, model inference, and real-time indexing.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven ingestion (Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda)
- Data discovery and governance (Glue Data Catalog, Lake Formation)
- Privacy enforcement (PII redaction policies)
- Real-time ML (SageMaker endpoint) for anomaly scores
- Indexing and lineage (DynamoDB, S3, Glue)
- Security and cost controls (IAM roles, KMS, logging, retention)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example Lambda snippet for redacting emails in incoming JSON
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const payload = JSON.parse(event.body || '{}');
  if (payload.email) {
    payload.email = '***@***.***';
  }
  // forward to next stage (e.g., Kinesis / S3)
  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(payload) };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotent writes across the pipeline?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect redaction failures or data drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a compliant, scalable machine-learning data pipeline with end-to-end governance and drift controls.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven orchestration (S3/EventBridge/Step Functions)
- Privacy: KMS, encryption at rest, access logs
- Data lineage: Glue Data Catalog
- Governance: Lake Formation
- ML inference: SageMaker endpoint, model drift monitoring
- Cross-account sharing and cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js: SageMaker endpoint invocation sketch
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function classify(input){
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'ml-endpoint', Body: JSON.stringify(input), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return JSON.parse(res.Body.toString());
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design drift detection metrics and alerting for the SageMaker model?
- What rollback strategy would you implement if the endpoint drifts or data governance policy changes?

